(*
ShipsBellAlarmNodeC6 — the same bell, triggered by the RTC instead of by a poll.

Design: docs/design/2026-08-03-c6-shipsbell-always-on-node-design.md, with the
trigger taken from §16.4 of zephyr/boards/shipsBells/2026-07-26-shipsbellsv3-design.md.
Sibling of `ShipsBellNodeC6.nsl`, which stays exactly as it is: that node is the
POLL demonstration and this one is the INTERRUPT demonstration, and deleting either
deletes the aspect it demonstrates (D10).

THE DIVISION THIS NODE EXISTS TO SHOW: **the RTC says when; the MCU's own timer only
ever says "the RTC didn't."** The MCU timebase never competes as a trigger, which is
the entire reason for fitting a DS3231. The poll version measured the cost of the
other arrangement: across one day its strikes walked from 174 ms to 465 ms after the
boundary — about 15 ppm of MCU crystal against the RTC, visible as a drift and not
as jitter (2026-08-04).

THREE SOURCES, ONE REACTION. `clock/tick` is fed by:

  1. THE ALARM, at boundary − 5 s. DS3231 Alarm1 (Alarm2 has no seconds register),
     re-armed after every strike, delivered through the driver's own ISW workqueue.
  2. THE COUNTDOWN, a one-shot armed by (1) for the boundary itself. This is what
     actually strikes, so the strike instant is a local timer over 5 s rather than
     an I2C read in the interrupt path.
  3. THE WATCHDOG, a one-shot armed at each strike for the NEXT boundary + 2 s.

Nothing above `clock/tick` knows which one arrived. Every one of them does the same
thing: re-derive the slot from the clock and let the ratchet decide. That is D1's
amendment taken literally, and it is why adding a second trigger needs no new state.

WHY A WATCHDOG AND NOT A BACKSTOP POLL. §16.4 is right that "a missed edge is
silent", and the modes that can eat an edge are specific: a failed re-arm, or a
failed A2F clear — INT/SQW is a LEVEL, so if the flag is never cleared the line stays
asserted and the next alarm produces no new edge at all. Both have one signature: an
alarm we armed did not fire when it was due. That is a DEADLINE, and a deadline wants
a one-shot, not a repeater. A 60 s poll would ring up to 60 s late; this rings 2 s
late. And between boundaries the MCU holds exactly one armed timer and is otherwise
idle — the same posture as a pure interrupt, so §9's battery version is not
foreclosed by this design the way a poll would foreclose it.

The watchdog fires DURING the pattern in the healthy case (boundary + 2 s, against an
eight-bell pattern that runs 6.88 s). That is not a collision, and the reason is worth
stating because it is what makes 2 s honest rather than lucky: **the ratchet advances
`lastSlot` when the ring STARTS, not when it finishes**, so the watchdog's tick
derives a slot that has already been rung and does nothing. If the ratchet advanced at
the end, this grace would have to clear 6.88 s.

The watchdog is also the only DIAGNOSTIC here. When it fires and finds the boundary
unrung, that is positive evidence the alarm failed, and it says so. A poll could never
report that, because it would be ringing everything and the alarm's failure would be
invisible underneath.

BUILD IT WITH BOTH FRAGMENTS OR IT IS THE POLLING NODE WEARING THIS NAME:

  OEVM_NTP_SERVER6=fdc5:9109:9afe:97ea:9541:d963:f8d9:8567 \
  ./run.sh --board esp32c6 --node ShipsBellAlarmNodeC6.nsl \
           --conf-extra "prj_esp32c6_thread.conf;prj_esp32c6_rtcalarm.conf" \
           --pristine --flash

`OEVM_SOLENOID_CONSOLE=1` is ABSENT on purpose and must not be helpfully re-added
(rete#182). This is the unattended board, and the design's rule for it is
categorical: `strike_console` reads the console UART, so pulling the cable does not
make that input go quiet — it makes it go UNDRIVEN, and a floating RX is noise a
strike parser will happily act on. The product strike path is the nsl gpio seam;
the console striker is bench instrumentation that belongs on a bench. See
`boards/shipsBells/2026-07-26-shipsbellsv3-design.md` §22, which also records the
striker being suspected of a real chirping fault (#149) and cleared, and #178 —
a console cable disappearing from a bell, which is exactly the floating-RX
precondition. The failure it buys is not a build error: you get a working bell that
can also be rung by line noise, which reads as a haunted bell.

Without `prj_esp32c6_rtcalarm.conf` there is no `CONFIG_RTC_ALARM`, `rtcAlarm at:`
answers false, and the node runs on its watchdog alone — correct behaviour, wrong
node. So it PRINTS ITS TRIGGER at boot and names what is missing. That line exists
because this bench lost twenty minutes on 2026-08-04 to `OEVM_NTP_SERVER6` being
absent from a build and the node correctly, quietly, carrying on without it.

Dialect: `[ ... ] whileTrue:`, never `repeat`. No modulo in nsl — every one is
spelled `n - ((n // d) * d)`. State lives in instance slots. ONE `Timers` instance,
held in a slot — tidiness since rete#244 made the accessor a singleton, though
`dispatch:` still silently drops a token it does not know.

expect (C6 console, wall-powered):
shipsbell(alarm) ready, slot 1800 s, lead 5 s, watchdog +2 s, 10/500/1600 ms
rtcalarm: armed 14:59:55
bell slot 30 at 15:00:00 — 6 bells
*)
class ShipsBellAlarmNodeC6 usingPlatform: platform net: net = (
  | plat space timers tick bellCh bridge beatCh spans
    lastSlot. striking ::= false. strokeCount ::= 0. strokeIndex ::= 0. pinOn ::= false.
    haveClock ::= false. gpioMissing ::= false. midnightPending ::= false.
    alarmOk ::= false. armedFor. checkIns ::= 0. | ) (

  (* --- proof of life (#56 D8, added 2026-08-05) -----------------------------
     This node speaks once per HALF HOUR, and `udpsink` cannot tell a dead node
     from a quiet one — a UDP listener has no peer to lose, so it reports
     `port=up` forever. On 2026-08-05 that cost two hours of silence which read
     as four ordinary gaps. A check-in on porta's cadence makes absence
     detectable, and carries `sys/metrics` for free.

     D8 of the metrics design (2026-08-04) scoped the bridge onto the gate node
     and wrote down exactly what deferring it cost: "the node with metrics is the
     gate node, and the node that runs for days has none." This is that debt paid,
     and the class header had to change to acquire `net:` — a shade more than the
     "constructor call" D8 estimated.

     CADENCE IS 30 s, NOT the bridge node's 2 s, and the number is DERIVED rather
     than picked: it only has to be well inside this node's own 30-minute quiet
     gap for silence to be detectable, and nothing here needs finer. At 2 s it
     would be 900 check-ins per gap, forever, on a mesh that also carries a bell. *)
  (* rete#186: THE ID IS THE BOARD, and it is baked at build time rather than written
     here. This method used to answer a string literal, and the literal was the bug:
     whatever devkit was flashed with this app wrote into that one space, so `c6-bench`
     accumulated 36,153 events across 19 `obs/build` entries from at least two boards
     AND two different apps, with nothing in the store saying which. `platform nodeId`
     answers the bench instance the image was built for (zephyr/run.sh fills it from the
     same collector env file the console reader resolves through), so the flash, the
     console and the porta space cannot disagree about who this is.
     The ROLE is not gone, it just lives where it always belonged: porta writes
     `goal/name` and this node converges `obs/name` onto it. *)
  nodeId    = ( ^ platform nodeId )
  gwAddr    = ( ^ 'fdc5:9109:9afe:97ea:9541:d963:f8d9:8567' )
  gwPort    = ( ^ 6969 )
  localPort = ( ^ 16971 )   (* NOT 16970 — PortaBridgeNodeC6 owns that one *)
  cadence   = ( ^ 30000 )
  chip      = ( ^ #esp32c6 )

  (* --- boot --------------------------------------------------------------- *)

  public main: platform2 args: args = (
    | sock |
    plat:: platform.
    space:: plat space.

    timers:: plat timers.                (* HOLD ONE — see the header *)
    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :e | timers dispatch: e ].

    (* ONE reaction, three sources: the alarm, the countdown, the watchdog. *)
    tick:: space channel: 'clock/tick'.
    tick whenever: '' do: [ :m | self onTick ].

    bellCh:: space channel: 'bell/stroke'.
    bellCh whenever: '' do: [ :m | self strokeStep ].

    (* The bridge. A bind failure is NOT fatal here, unlike on the gate node: this
       node's job is to ring on time, and it must keep ringing with no gateway,
       no mesh and no report. Silence about itself is a degradation; a bell that
       stops for want of a socket is a regression. *)
    sock:: net udpBindPort: self localPort.
    sock == nil
      ifTrue:  [ 'porta: bind FAILED — ringing on, reporting nothing' out ]
      ifFalse: [
        (* The bridge registers six span slots of its own at construction and so
           needs a Spans. This node takes no app-level spans, so the instance
           exists only to hold the bridge's name<->slot table — but it must be ONE
           instance, and it must be this one. *)
        spans:: plat Spans new.
        bridge:: plat PortaBridge
          socket: sock space: space id: self nodeId peer: self gwAddr port: self gwPort
          spans: spans.
        (* rete#231 D2: this node's own promise about its radio. The budget is
           8 x the cadence — #215's measured slack, chosen HERE by the node rather
           than by the gateway, because the node is the only party that knows its
           own loss rate. 30 s is this node's beat, and the check-in rides it. *)
        bridge liveness: self cadence budget: self cadence * 8.
        (* rete#259: the bridge owns obs/build; #man rides iff the image
           carries a manifest pin (rete#243). *)
        bridge publishBuild: self chip.
        beatCh:: space channel: 'sys/tick'.
        beatCh whenever: '' do: [ :m | self checkIn ].
        (* rete#231 D1: the KERNEL owns the period. This node's beat IS its
           check-in, and the registration moving into the bridge is what puts it
           in timer_target_snapshot as the bridge's own deadline — a bell that
           wedges between strokes now stops emitting a heartbeat instead of
           emitting one from a node that is not ringing. *)
        bridge startTicking: timers on: beatCh.
        ('porta: check-in every ' , (self cadence // 1000) printString ,
          ' s as ' , self nodeId) out ].

    ('shipsbell(alarm) ready, slot ' , self slotSeconds printString ,
      ' s, lead ' , self leadSeconds printString ,
      ' s, watchdog +' , (self graceMillis // 1000) printString , ' s, ' ,
      self widthMillis printString , '/' , self pairGapMillis printString , '/' ,
      self setGapMillis printString , ' ms') out.

    self syncClock.
    self showClock.

    (* Seed and arm. Nothing rings at boot (D4). *)
    self seedAndArm.

    [ true ] whileTrue: [ plat pumpEvents. space drain ].
  )

  (* --- proof of life ------------------------------------------------------
     ONE check-in. Guarded on `striking`, and that guard is load-bearing rather
     than tidy: `checkIn` is a UDP round trip with a budget, and blocking inside a
     reaction while a pattern is in flight stretches the gap between strokes. A
     missed check-in is idempotent — the next one carries the same cells, by
     cursor — but a bell that pauses mid-pattern is audibly wrong, and this node's
     one job is to sound right. Reporting yields to ringing.

     A raised checkIn must not take the node down either: the bell outranks its
     own telemetry, so the failure is counted-and-carried rather than propagated. *)
  checkIn = (
    bridge == nil ifTrue: [ ^ self ].
    striking ifTrue: [ ^ self ].
    [ bridge checkIn. checkIns:: checkIns + 1 ]
      on: [ :e | true ]
      do: [ :e | 'porta: check-in raised — ringing on' out ].

    (* PRINT EVERY CHECK-IN, and print the bridge's own counters with it. The first
       cut of this method printed only on failure, which made a working check-in
       and a silently-missing one look identical on the console — the exact blind
       spot this whole change exists to close, rebuilt inside the fix for it. A
       proof of life that cannot be distinguished from silence is not one.
       `misses` is the load-bearing number: it rising means the datagram left and
       no reply came back, which separates "the mesh ate it" from "the node never
       tried". *)
    ('porta: check-in ' , checkIns printString ,
      ' misses ' , bridge misses printString ,
      ' cursor ' , bridge cursor printString ,
      ' acked ' , bridge acked printString) out.
    ^ self )

  (* --- the schedule ------------------------------------------------------- *)

  (* Every tick, whatever produced it. Re-derives the slot and lets the ratchet
     decide; a tick with nothing to do is the healthy case, not an error. *)
  onTick = (
    | secs slot |
    secs:: plat wallClock.
    secs == nil ifTrue: [ ^ self noClock ].

    haveClock ifFalse: [
      haveClock:: true.
      ('clock ' , (self hms: secs) , ' — readings good') out ].

    slot:: secs // self slotSeconds.

    lastSlot == nil ifTrue: [
      lastSlot:: slot.
      ('seed slot ' , slot printString , ' at ' , (self hms: secs) ,
        ' — nothing rung') out.
      ^ self armAfter: secs ].

    (self advancedTo: slot from: lastSlot) ifTrue: [
      (* A boundary is behind us. Either the countdown just delivered us onto it,
         or the alarm never came and this is the watchdog earning its keep. *)
      (armedFor == slot) ifFalse: [
        ('watchdog: no alarm for slot ' , slot printString , ' — ringing late') out ].
      lastSlot:: slot.
      ^ self ringSlot: slot at: secs ].

    (* Not a boundary. If one is close, this was the alarm's early wake: count
       down to it exactly. Guarded on `armedFor` so a second early tick does not
       arm a second countdown for the same boundary. *)
    ((self secondsToBoundary: secs) <= self leadSeconds) ifTrue: [
      ^ self armCountdownAt: secs slot: slot ].
    ^ self )

  (* THE RATCHET, and the midnight case (D4, D5) — identical to the poll node's,
     deliberately: the trigger changed, the schedule did not. *)
  advancedTo: slot from: prev = (
    slot = prev  ifTrue: [ ^ false ].
    slot > prev  ifTrue: [ ^ true ].
    ^ slot = 0 )

  ringSlot: slot at: secs = (
    | n |
    n:: (plat bell) bellsForHalfHour: slot.
    ('bell slot ' , slot printString , ' at ' , (self hms: secs) , ' — ' ,
      n printString , (self bellWord: n)) out.
    slot = 0 ifTrue: [ midnightPending:: true ].
    self startPattern: n.
    (* Arm the NEXT boundary from here, so a strike and its successor's arming are
       one event. The watchdog armed below fires 2 s into this very pattern in the
       healthy case and finds the slot already rung. *)
    ^ self armAfter: secs )

  (* --- arming: the alarm, the watchdog, the countdown ---------------------- *)

  seedAndArm = (
    | secs |
    secs:: plat wallClock.
    secs == nil ifTrue: [ ^ self noClock ].
    lastSlot:: secs // self slotSeconds.
    ('seed slot ' , lastSlot printString , ' at ' , (self hms: secs) ,
      ' — nothing rung') out.
    ^ self armAfter: secs )

  (* Arm both deadlines for the next boundary after `secs`. The RTC alarm is the
     trigger; the watchdog only ever says the alarm did not come. *)
  armAfter: secs = (
    | toBoundary |
    armedFor:: nil.
    toBoundary:: self secondsToBoundary: secs.

    (* The alarm, at boundary - lead. Wrapped into the day, because 00:00 minus a
       lead is 23:59:55 and the part matches a time-of-day, not an offset. *)
    alarmOk:: (plat rtcAlarm) at: (self wrapDay: secs + toBoundary - self leadSeconds)
                             post: #alarm on: tick.
    alarmOk ifFalse: [
      'trigger=WATCHDOG ONLY — rtcAlarm refused (image built without prj_esp32c6_rtcalarm.conf?)' out ].

    (* The watchdog, at boundary + grace. Never cancelled: it is cheaper to let it
       fire into a ratchet that has nothing to do than to carry cancel state. *)
    timers after: (toBoundary * 1000) + self graceMillis post: #watchdog on: tick.
    ^ self )

  (* The alarm's early wake. One local one-shot for the remaining milliseconds,
     plus a small margin so the RTC's second has certainly rolled over when the
     countdown re-reads it — wallClock has 1 s resolution, and firing 1 ms early
     would read the OLD slot and ring nothing until the watchdog. *)
  armCountdownAt: secs slot: slot = (
    (armedFor == nil) ifFalse: [ ^ self ].
    armedFor:: slot + 1.
    timers after: ((self secondsToBoundary: secs) * 1000) + self strikeMarginMillis
           post: #countdown on: tick.
    ^ self )

  (* Seconds from `secs` to the NEXT boundary. At a boundary this is a FULL slot,
     never 0 — the same edge 05_ntp_catchup.nsl pins, and for the same reason: 0
     would arm a one-shot that fires immediately and strikes the slot twice. *)
  secondsToBoundary: secs = (
    ^ self slotSeconds - (secs - ((secs // self slotSeconds) * self slotSeconds)) )

  wrapDay: secs = (
    secs < 0     ifTrue: [ ^ secs + 86400 ].
    secs > 86399 ifTrue: [ ^ secs - 86400 ].
    ^ secs )

  (* D7. Silence is the correct failure mode for a bell; a guess is not. *)
  noClock = (
    haveClock ifTrue: [
      haveClock:: false.
      'clock nil — no DS3231 reading; not striking until it returns' out ].
    ^ self )

  (* --- the striker (identical to the poll node's) --------------------------- *)

  startPattern: n = (
    striking ifTrue: [
      ('bells ' , n printString , ' REFUSED — a pattern is already in flight') out.
      ^ self ].
    ((n < 1) or: [ n > 8 ]) ifTrue: [
      ('bells ' , n printString , ' REFUSED (allowed 1..8)') out.
      ^ self ].
    strokeCount:: n.
    strokeIndex:: 0.
    striking:: true.
    ^ self strikeOn )

  strokeStep = (
    pinOn ifTrue: [ ^ self strikeOff ].
    ^ self strikeOn )

  strikeOn = (
    strokeIndex:: strokeIndex + 1.
    pinOn:: true.
    self pin: true.
    timers after: self widthMillis post: #stroke on: bellCh.
    ^ self )

  strikeOff = (
    pinOn:: false.
    self pin: false.
    strokeIndex = strokeCount ifTrue: [
      striking:: false.
      'bells done' out.
      ^ self afterPattern ].
    timers after: (self gapAfter: strokeIndex) post: #stroke on: bellCh.
    ^ self )

  gapAfter: i = (
    (i - ((i // 2) * 2)) = 1 ifTrue: [ ^ self pairGapMillis ].
    ^ self setGapMillis )

  afterPattern = (
    midnightPending ifTrue: [
      midnightPending:: false.
      self syncClock.
      self showClock ].
    ^ self )

  pin: aBool = (
    [ (plat gpio) set: self strikePin to: aBool ]
      on: [ :e | e tag = #gpioUndeclared ]
      do: [ :e |
        gpioMissing ifFalse: [
          gpioMissing:: true.
          ('bell: GPIO' , self strikePin printString ,
            ' not declared on this board — keeping time, ringing nothing') out ] ].
    ^ self )

  (* --- the clock ---------------------------------------------------------- *)

  syncClock = (
    | secs date rc |
    secs:: plat ntpSyncTz: self tzOffset.
    secs == nil ifTrue: [ 'ntp miss — keeping the RTC' out. ^ self ].
    date:: plat ntpDate.
    date == nil ifTrue: [ 'ntp date miss — keeping the RTC' out. ^ self ].
    rc:: plat wallClockSetDate: date time: secs.
    rc == nil ifTrue: [ 'rtc write REFUSED — keeping the RTC' out. ^ self ].
    ('ntp ok ' , date printString , ' ' , (self hms: secs)) out.
    ^ self )

  showClock = (
    | secs |
    secs:: plat wallClock.
    secs == nil
      ifTrue:  [ 'rtc nil (no DS3231, or never set)' out ]
      ifFalse: [ ('rtc ' , (self hms: secs) , '  slot ' ,
                   (secs // self slotSeconds) printString) out ].
    ^ self )

  hms: secs = (
    | h rem m s |
    h::   secs // 3600.
    rem:: secs - (h * 3600).
    m::   rem // 60.
    s::   rem - (m * 60).
    ^ (self pad: h) , ':' , (self pad: m) , ':' , (self pad: s) )

  pad: n = ( ^ n < 10 ifTrue: [ '0' , n printString ] ifFalse: [ n printString ] )

  bellWord: n = ( ^ n = 1 ifTrue: [ ' bell' ] ifFalse: [ ' bells' ] )

  (* --- constants ---------------------------------------------------------- *)

  slotSeconds        = ( ^ 1800 )
  leadSeconds        = ( ^ 5 )       (* alarm fires here before the boundary *)
  graceMillis        = ( ^ 2000 )    (* watchdog, after the boundary *)
  strikeMarginMillis = ( ^ 200 )     (* countdown overshoot; see armCountdownAt: *)

  strikePin          = ( ^ 18 )
  widthMillis        = ( ^ 10 )
  pairGapMillis      = ( ^ 500 )
  setGapMillis       = ( ^ 1600 )

  tzOffset           = ( ^ -25200 )
) : (
)
